Alternately, `!` could be disallowed as an inline asm input entirely, but that would be a breaking change and need a crater run, since

```rs
fn foo() {
 unsafe {
  std::arch::asm!(
   "/* {} */",
   in(reg) panic!(),
  );
 }
}
```

is allowed on stable ([playground](https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&mode=debug&edition=2024&gist=3c632f45f608b1f93c5293873a062f72))

I think the assembly is not checked if the inline asm doesn't reach the codegen backend for any reason (whether it is optimized out before reaching the codegen backend, or is never codegenned because it is in an `#[inline(always)]` function that isn't called in the current crate, e.g.), not just if it has a `!` input.

E.g. these both compile ([playground](https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&mode=debug&edition=2024&gist=57258c0b2899739423db95e73b9a5beb)):

```rs
#[inline(never)]
pub fn bar() {
    return; // makes the asm statically unreachable
    unsafe { core::arch::asm!("foo bar baz"); }
}
#[inline(never)]
pub fn foo() {
    if true { return; }
    unsafe { core::arch::asm!("foo bar baz"); } // `SimplifyCfg-final` MIR pass deletes the block with the asm,
    // and even if you disable it with `-Zmir-enable-passes=-SimplifyCfg-final`,
    // `codegen_mir` still seems to notice the block is unreachable and doesn't codegen it to LLVM IR (see links)
}
```
([godbolt link](https://rust.godbolt.org/z/PG4ffjrzd), [`codegen_mir` source link](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/main/compiler/rustc_codegen_ssa/src/mir/mod.rs#L391))

This compiles in release mode but not debug mode ([playground](https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&mode=release&edition=2024&gist=2f8a54ac1776a50e51211b10bbae5583)):

```rs
#[inline(never)]
pub fn foo() {
    if 3 + 4 == 7 { return }
    unsafe { core::arch::asm!("foo bar baz"); }
}
```

If you add `black_box` around the `if` conditions, then the above examples' assembly does reach the codegen backend and fail to compile in debug and release mode.


This compiles in debug mode and release mode ([playground](https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&mode=debug&edition=2024&gist=67a1580fecdde8ad3d0194a57971119d)), but will fail to compile if a dependency *calls* `foo` (in such a way that reaches codegen):

```rs
#[inline(always)]
pub fn foo() {
    unsafe { core::arch::asm!("foo bar baz"); }
}
```

----

I'm not super familiar with LLVM, but it seems like even if the malformed asm does reach the codegen backend, if LLVM determines that the asm is unreachable, it might optimize it out before checking it too: ([godbolt link](https://llvm.godbolt.org/z/ehqYhhE83))

```llvm
define void @foo() unnamed_addr #0 {
start:
  ret void // comment out this and next line to fail to compile
nver: // comment out this and previous line to fail to compile
  tail call void asm sideeffect alignstack inteldialect "foo bar baz", "~{dirflag},~{fpsr},~{flags},~{memory}"() #1
  ret void
}

attributes #0 = { noinline nounwind nonlazybind uwtable "probe-stack"="inline-asm" "target-cpu"="x86-64" }
attributes #1 = { nounwind }
```

So even if we do change our MIR optimizations and codegen to make sure unreachable inline asm reaches codegen, LLVM still might not check it.

Could you please elaborate on how you used AI? Did you follow the [LLM policy](https://forge.rust-lang.org/policies/llm-usage.html)?

I would also like to see a regression uitest added to this PR. See the testing chapter of the rustc dev guide for help on creating such a test: https://rustc-dev-guide.rust-lang.org/tests/intro.html
